    </ul>

    <!-- ==================== Q&A SECTION ==================== -->
    <h2>🎓 Teacher / Viva Presentation Q&A Cheat Sheet</h2>

    <div class="qa-box">
        <div class="qa-q">Q1: How did you test your Tic-Tac-Toe application?</div>
        <div class="qa-a">
            <strong>Answer:</strong> We implemented an automated unit testing suite using <strong>JUnit 5</strong> in <code>GameLogicTest.java</code>. We wrote 8 automated test cases covering valid move execution, out-of-bounds / occupied cell rejection, all 8 winning lines (3 rows, 3 columns, 2 diagonals), 9-move draw condition, multi-round score accumulation, and score resetting. Running <code>mvn test</code> executes all 8 tests with a 100% pass rate.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-box">
        <div class="qa-q">Q2: What is the purpose of the <code>v1.0-tested</code> Git tag in Week 8?</div>
        <div class="qa-a">
            <strong>Answer:</strong> Git tags create permanent milestone bookmarks in the repository's history. The <code>v1.0-tested</code> tag marks the exact commit where all core game logic and automated test suites passed 100% verification before moving to submission documentation.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-box">
        <div class="qa-q">Q3: How do match scores persist across multiple rounds without a database?</div>
        <div class="qa-a">
            <strong>Answer:</strong> The <code>GameLogic</code> object is stored inside the user's <code>HttpSession</code>. When a round ends and the player clicks "New Round", the servlet calls <code>game.resetBoard()</code>, which clears the 3&times;3 matrix and resets turns, but preserves <code>xWins</code>, <code>oWins</code>, and <code>draws</code> throughout the browser session.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-box">
        <div class="qa-q">Q4: What is the Post-Redirect-Get (PRG) pattern and why is it used?</div>
        <div class="qa-a">
            <strong>Answer:</strong> When a user clicks a cell, the browser sends an HTTP POST to <code>GameServlet</code>. After updating the game state, instead of forwarding directly to the JSP, the servlet issues an HTTP 302 redirect (<code>response.sendRedirect("index.jsp")</code>). This forces the browser to load <code>index.jsp</code> via HTTP GET, preventing accidental duplicate moves or browser warning prompts when the page is refreshed.
        </div>
    </div>

